// Practice quiz: Navigating files and folders in the shell
// Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: git-linux-ai-foundations/linux-foundations/navigating-the-shell

::o1 hidden names::Your home folder holds a hidden file named .bashrc. Plain ls does not show it. Which command lists it as well? {
=ls -a #Correct. Names that start with a dot are hidden from plain ls; ls -a shows them too, plus . and ...
~pwd #Not quite. pwd prints the path of the folder you are in; it does not list what the folder holds.
~cd .bashrc #Not quite. cd moves into a folder; .bashrc is a file, and cd lists nothing.
~ls .. #Not quite. ls .. lists the parent folder, and it still hides dot names.
}

::o1 where am I::You have run several cd commands and are no longer sure which folder you are in. Which command tells you? {
=pwd #Correct. pwd prints the full path of the current (working) folder, starting from /.
~ls #Not quite. ls lists what is inside the current folder, not where that folder is.
~cd #Not quite. cd with no argument moves you to your home folder; it does not report where you were.
~ls -a #Not quite. ls -a lists entries including hidden ones; it does not print your location.
}

::o2 absolute or relative::You are in /home/sam. Which path means the same folder wherever you type it from? {
=/var/log #Correct. A path that starts with / is absolute: it is read from the root, so your current folder does not matter.
~logs #Not quite. A bare name is relative: it is read from the folder you are in.
~../log #Not quite. .. means the parent of the current folder, so the result depends on where you are.
~./log #Not quite. . means the current folder, so this is relative too.
}

::o2 home and parent::You are in /home/sam/work/app. Which command takes you to /home/sam/notes? {
=cd \~/notes #Correct. \~ stands for your home folder, /home/sam, so \~/notes is /home/sam/notes from anywhere.
~cd ../notes #Not quite. One .. reaches /home/sam/work, which has no notes folder.
~cd /notes #Not quite. A leading / starts at the root, so this looks for /notes, which is a different folder.
~cd notes #Not quite. A bare name is looked up inside the current folder, /home/sam/work/app.
}

::o3 a name with a space::The current folder holds a folder named My Projects, with a space in the name. Which command moves you into it? {
=cd "My Projects" #Correct. The quotes keep the name as one word, so cd receives a single argument. cd My\\ Projects also works.
~cd My Projects #Not quite. The space splits the name into two arguments, so bash cd fails and you stay where you are.
~cd My-Projects #Not quite. That names a different folder, My-Projects.
~cd /My Projects #Not quite. The / starts at the root, and the space still splits the name.
}
